  "answer_text": "Great question! Making eye contact with someone who has a lazy eye (also called strabismus or amblyopia) can feel awkward at first, but with a few simple tips, it becomes much easier. Here's what you should know:\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Look at the Eye That's Looking at You**\nLook at the eye which is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be rude, it may feel to them as though you are staring at their \"wonky\" eye.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Remember \u2014 Both Eyes Are Actually Focused on You**\nWhat can throw you off is that it looks like one of their eyes is looking at you and one is looking somewhere else. That can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact. Keep in mind that both their eyes are, in fact, looking at you \u2014 one just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Don't Overthink It \u2014 Just Carry On**\nFor most people with a lazy eye, they feel like they're staring right at you. So don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Try the \"Soft Focus\" or Blurring Technique**\nIf there's something unusual on someone's face that you don't want to stare at rudely, blurring your eyes lets you look straight at them and listen better. This means slightly relaxing your gaze so you're not fixating on one specific eye.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **It's Okay to Shift Your Focus Between Eyes**\nGive yourself permission to look at the non-dominant eye once in a while. Much like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them and other things about that person start to form your dominant image.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **If It's Someone Close to You, It's Okay to Acknowledge It**\nIf you can't concentrate because of your friend's situation, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. Saying something like *\"Sometimes your eye starts moving and it throws me off\"* can lighten a possibly awkward situation. Don't use this approach with a stranger or someone you'll rarely see again \u2014 just move on. It's not worth making them feel self-conscious.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Focus on Another Part of Their Face if Needed**\nIf you really feel uneasy, find another point of their face to focus on and wrap up the conversation as quickly as needed.\n\n---\n\nThe most important takeaway is to **be natural and respectful**. People with a lazy eye are very aware of it and appreciate when others treat them normally without making it a big deal.",
